occurs because in C#, there are two interfaces named `IList`:

- `System.Collections.IList` (non-generic)
- `System.Collections.Generic.IList<T>` (generic, requires a type parameter)

When you write `var list = (IList)obj;` without specifying which `IList` you mean, and especially if you have `using System.Collections.Generic;` at the top, the compiler may interpret `IList` as the generic version, which requires a type argument (like `IList<int>`). Since you didn't provide one, the compiler throws an error.

### 2. Does it make sense that it is faulty? What is the logic behind the issue?

Yes, it makes sense. The compiler needs to know which interface you mean. If you want to work with lists of any type dynamically (without knowing the element type at compile time), you must use the non-generic `System.Collections.IList`, which works with `object` elements. The generic `IList<T>` cannot be used without specifying the type parameter.

### 3. Functional Version, Motivation, and Why It Works

To fix the issue, you should explicitly use the non-generic `System.Collections.IList` interface. All `List<T>` types implement this interface, so you can cast any list to it and access elements by index.

