Marshalling a `HashSet<string>` to bytes at a very low level (i.e., copying its raw memory layout) is not feasible or safe in C#, because `HashSet<T>` is a managed, reference-type collection with a complex, implementation-dependent internal structure. Its memory layout is not fixed, and it contains references to strings and internal arrays, which cannot be safely or portably copied as raw bytes.

If your goal is to serialize the contents of a `HashSet<string>` into a byte array (and later reconstruct it), the best approach is to serialize the logical contents, not the raw memory. Here is a simple, low-level way to do this manually, without relying on .NET's built-in serializers:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;

public static class HashSetStringSerializer
{
    public static byte[] Serialize(HashSet<string> set)
    {
        if (set == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(set));
        var bytes = new List<byte>();
        bytes.AddRange(BitConverter.GetBytes(set.Count));
        foreach (var s in set)
        {
            if (s == null) throw new InvalidOperationException("Null strings not supported.");
            var strBytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(s);
            bytes.AddRange(BitConverter.GetBytes(strBytes.Length));
            bytes.AddRange(strBytes);
        }
        return bytes.ToArray();
    }

    public static HashSet<string> Deserialize(byte[] data)
    {
        if (data == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(data));
        int pos = 0;
        int count = BitConverter.ToInt32(data, pos);
        pos += 4;
        var set = new HashSet<string>();
        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
        {
            int len = BitConverter.ToInt32(data, pos);
            pos += 4;
            var strBytes = new byte[len];
            Buffer.BlockCopy(data, pos, strBytes, 0, len);
            pos += len;
            set.Add(Encoding.UTF8.GetString(strBytes));
        }
        return set;
    }
}
```

