There are two possible interpretations:
1. The steady-state can be any point along the baseline (continuous).
2. The steady-state must be one of the discrete N points (discrete mapping).

**MATLAB code for both interpretations:**

**A. Continuous projection (returns the foot of the perpendicular):**
```matlab
function Q = estimateSteadyStateContinuous(steadyPoints, P)
    % steadyPoints: N x 2 matrix, first and last row define the baseline
    % P: 1 x 2 vector, the point to estimate
    A = steadyPoints(1, :);
    B = steadyPoints(end, :);
    AB = B - A;
    AP = P - A;
    t = dot(AP, AB) / dot(AB, AB);
    % Optionally clamp t to [0,1] if only the segment is valid
    t = max(0, min(1, t));
    Q = A + t * AB;
end
```

**B. Discrete mapping (returns the nearest steady-state point):**
```matlab
function idx = estimateSteadyStateDiscrete(steadyPoints, P)
    % steadyPoints: N x 2 matrix
    % P: 1 x 2 vector
    A = steadyPoints(1, :);
    B = steadyPoints(end, :);
    AB = B - A;
    AP = P - A;
    t = dot(AP, AB) / dot(AB, AB);
    t = max(0, min(1, t));
    N = size(steadyPoints, 1);
    idx = round(1 + (N-1)*t);
    idx = max(1, min(N, idx));
    % The corresponding steady-state point is steadyPoints(idx, :)
end
